WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.137
Grouped statistics let us compare categories numerically.

00:00:04.137 --> 00:00:12.452
In this example, we calculate count, mean, median, and standard deviation for each region.

00:00:12.452 --> 00:00:18.945
Count is important because a group with very few observations can produce an unstable average.

00:00:19.448 --> 00:00:27.041
Mean and median describe the center in different ways, while standard deviation tells us about within-group variability.

00:00:27.041 --> 00:00:29.850
Run the code and compare the regions.

00:00:29.850 --> 00:00:33.636
Ask whether the mean and median tell a similar story.

00:00:33.636 --> 00:00:39.326
If they are far apart, the group may contain skewness or unusual values.

00:00:39.326 --> 00:00:45.345
Also be careful not to make strong population claims from a very small teaching dataset.

00:00:45.345 --> 00:00:49.670
At this stage, we are practicing descriptive comparison.

00:00:49.670 --> 00:00:53.431
Formal inference requires additional assumptions and methods.
